Q: C# How to represent and traverse an N-Ary tree A: <p>It seems that you don't need any &quot;special&quot; datastructure - just a class containing a collection of itself:</p>
<pre><code>class HierarchyMember
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public List&lt;HierarchyMember&gt; Children { get; set; }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And I suppose any popular json serializer for C# should handle that. For example Newtonsoft's Json.NET:</p>
<pre><code>Console.WriteLine(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(new HierarchyMember
{
    Name = &quot;Root&quot;,
    Children = new List&lt;HierarchyMember&gt;
    {
        new HierarchyMember {Name = &quot;Child_1&quot;}, 
        new HierarchyMember {Name = &quot;Child_2&quot;}
    }
}, Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.Indented));
</code></pre>
<p>Prints:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;Root&quot;,
  &quot;Children&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;Child_1&quot;,
      &quot;Children&quot;: null
    },
    {
      &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;Child_2&quot;,
      &quot;Children&quot;: null
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>

Q: check each array with each array - and not doubling A: <p>Yes you can use <code>sets</code> and <code>map</code> function to implement this for e.g like this</p>
<p>Assuming you have 2D arraylist</p>
<pre><code>let arr = [[1, 2, 4], [4, 9, 10], [7, 8, 11], [2, 30, 50]]

let sets = arr.map { Set($0) }

for firstIdx in 0..&lt;sets.count {
    for secondIdx in (firstIdx+1)..&lt;sets.count {
        if !sets[firstIdx].isDisjoint(with: sets[secondIdx]) {
            print(&quot;Found matching element between one array at index \(firstIdx) and another array at index \(secondIdx)&quot;)
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
